update history_articles
set
  title = 'A tradition of Guru Arjan Dev Ji',
  excerpt = 'Two published tellings connect ਮੱਲੂ ਨੰਗਲ with Guru Arjan and a Sikh remembered as Mallu. They do not agree, and neither is a contemporary record.',
  approx_year = 'Undated; Guru Arjan’s years are 1581–1606',
  image = '/images/gurdwara.jpg',
  is_demo = false,
  author = 'Archive editor',
  updated_at = '2026-10-14',
  sources = 'Punjab Kosh, vol. 2 (Language Department, Punjab), as reprinted on Punjabi Pedia, citing Ajit, 7 August 1992, and the 1981 Amritsar district handbook. Second telling: an online account of Gurdwara Shri Guru Arjan Dev Ji Sahib, Mallu Nangal, attributed to HistoricalGurudwaras.com and copied into this archive on 14 October 2026; that page could not be re-opened here. Nearby shrine, not this village: Kahn Singh / Encyclopaedia of Sikhism, “Ghukkevali”; Discover Sikhism, Gurdwara Sri Guru Ka Bagh Ghukeywali. Guru Arjan’s life: standard Sikh chronology (birth 1563, guruship 1581–1606, death in Mughal custody at Lahore, 1606).',
  body = $trad$This card keeps two things apart. The life of Sri Guru Arjan Dev Ji is documented well beyond this village. A halt at ਮੱਲੂ ਨੰਗਲ is local tradition. The two written versions of that tradition do not match, so they are printed side by side and not stitched into one story.

Sri Guru Arjan Dev Ji (1563–1606) was the fifth Sikh Guru, from 1581 until his death in Mughal custody at Lahore in 1606. In those years the sarovar and Harmandir Sahib at Amritsar were completed, and the Adi Granth was compiled and installed there in 1604. Those facts belong to Sikh history at large. They are not, by themselves, proof that he stopped in this village.

Punjab Kosh, volume 2, records one sakhi. Gurdwara Mallu Nangal Sahib stands outside the village, on the road from Amritsar toward Jagdev Kalan. The village is placed in Ajnala tehsil, about ten kilometres from Majitha. The Kosh says that Guru Arjan, travelling toward Guru Ke Bagh with devotees, rested here and asked for water. A servant drew it from a nearby well in a clay pot. A Pathan of the settlement then called Lille broke the pot, the water spilled, and the Guru remained thirsty. The earlier name of the place, in this telling, was Lille. The Guru is quoted as saying: “ਜਾਣਗੇ ਲਿੱਲੇ, ਆਉਣਗੇ ਗਿੱਲੇ” — the Lille will go; the Gills will come. A Gursikh named Mallu Gill, grazing buffaloes outside the village, gathered about twenty to twenty-five Sikhs that same night. The Pathans left. In the morning, pleased, the Guru named the village ਮੱਲੂ ਨੰਗਲ after Mallu. The Kosh adds that most residents are of the Gill got, and describes a white-marble gurdwara, a tall Nishan Sahib, a small library, rooms for visitors, Gurpurab and Sangrand gatherings, and langar. Its population figure, 1,752, is the 1981 count, not the 2011 census.

A second telling was supplied to this archive from an online directory, HistoricalGurudwaras.com, under Gurdwara Shri Guru Arjan Dev Ji Sahib, Mallu Nangal. The archive could not re-open that page, so this version is filed as received. In it the earlier settlement is Kho Nangal, associated with Pathans called the Lile. Guru Arjan is said to have been travelling toward Guru Ka Bagh, accompanied by Raja Hari Sen of Suket. Sikhs were sent to a well for water; local Pathans damaged the pitcher and spoke disrespectfully; the Guru went on. Bhai Mallu Ji is placed at Jagdev Kalan, not in the herd outside Lille. After the incident he is said to have attacked and destroyed the older settlement and founded a new one, called Malu Nangal after him, with the Guru’s blessings. That account does not produce a contemporary writing in which Guru Arjan ordered the attack, and it gives no year.

The two tellings cannot both be exact. One earlier name is Lille; the other is Kho Nangal. One Mallu is a Gill already at the place, who acts the same night; the other comes from Jagdev Kalan and founds a new village afterwards. Until an older manuscript, a revenue spelling, or a named village witness is filed, this archive will not choose between them and will not assign a year.

A related shrine is documented nearby, and should not be confused with this village. Gurdwara Guru Ka Bagh at Ghukkevali (also spelled Ghukeywali), in Ajnala tahsil, about twelve kilometres east of Ajnala and about twenty kilometres from Amritsar, is recorded as a place where Guru Arjan stayed while travelling in the Majha. A sakhi told there concerns Raja Hari Sen of Suket, who came to the Guru at Amritsar and later returned with him to Guru Ka Bagh. Guru Tegh Bahadur is also associated with that garden, in 1664. Those pages do not name ਮੱਲੂ ਨੰਗਲ. They do show why a local memory of a journey “toward Guru Ka Bagh”, and of Raja Hari Sen, can arise in this countryside.

Bhai Mallu of this sakhi is not Bhai Mallu Saahi, a Sikh remembered from the time of Guru Angad. No published source found for this archive places Guru Nanak, Guru Hargobind, Guru Tegh Bahadur, or Guru Gobind Singh in ਮੱਲੂ ਨੰਗਲ itself.

What would settle more of this: the Granthi and the gurdwara committee’s own sakhi, in Punjabi, with the wording they use; any inscription or foundation date; older spellings in revenue papers and maps; and a genealogy for Mallu that can be checked against Jagdev Kalan. Those papers are invited on the Contribute page. The website is not the only copy that should exist.$trad$
where slug = 'early-settlement-placeholder';

update about_sections
set body = $trad$A local tradition, preserved in more than one written form, connects ਮੱਲੂ ਨੰਗਲ with Sri Guru Arjan Dev Ji and a Sikh remembered as Mallu or Bhai Mallu Ji. Punjab Kosh and a gurdwara-directory account do not agree on the earlier name of the settlement or on who Mallu was. Both are filed on the History page as tradition, not as a dated record. No year is assigned.

Gurdwara Mallu Nangal Sahib is described in Punjab Kosh as standing outside the village on the road toward Jagdev Kalan. This archive does not yet hold a photograph of that building, an inscription, or a foundation date. Families and the gurdwara committee are invited to supply those.

The documented life of Guru Arjan — Harmandir Sahib, the Adi Granth, and his death at Lahore in 1606 — is Sikh history of the region. It is not, by itself, proof of a halt in this village. Nearby, Gurdwara Guru Ka Bagh at Ghukkevali in Ajnala tahsil is a separate shrine associated with Guru Arjan and, later, Guru Tegh Bahadur. It is not this village’s gurdwara.$trad$
where slug = 'religious-heritage';
